Done. All three artifacts are on disk and verified.

**What was produced**

- `/app/active_users_report.json` — 346 objects (from 500 input users; statuses present were `active`/`inactive`/`pending`, only `active` kept), 2-space indented, sorted ascending by `username`, with `user_id`, `username`, `email`, `last_login` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), `role_count`, and `primary_role` (`null` for the 50 active users with an empty `roles` array).
- `/app/cmd.sh` — the exact jq command with redirection:
  `jq '[.[]|select(.status=="active")|{user_id:.id,username:.username,email:.email,last_login:(.last_login|split("T")[0]),role_count:(.roles|length),primary_role:(.roles[0]//null)}]|sort_by(.username)' /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json`
- `/app/jq_args.yaml` — `filter` plus a single `args` entry carrying the same filter, so the documented reconstruction command supplies jq exactly one filter positional.

**Verification**

- `sh /app/cmd.sh` regenerates the report byte-identical to the reference output (`cmp` clean, exit 0).
- The documented reconstruction `jq $(yq -r '.args[]' /app/jq_args.yaml) "$(yq -r '.filter' /app/jq_args.yaml)" /app/users.json > ...` also produces a byte-identical file.
- Structural assertions all pass: array of 346, all six keys present on every object, every `last_login` matches `^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$`, usernames sorted, and `primary_role` is `null` exactly when `role_count == 0`.

**Two constraints worth knowing**

- `yq` here is the Python yq wrapping jq; it rejects an unquoted YAML scalar beginning with `[`, so the filter is stored as a double-quoted scalar with `"` escaped as `\"`.
- Because the reconstruction command expands `$(yq -r '.args[]')` unquoted, any space inside an argument splits it and corrupts the filter. The filter is therefore written space-free (`.roles[0]//null` instead of an `if/then/else/end` block, no spaces after `:` or `,`). As a side effect jq prints one harmless `Could not open file …` line to stderr during the reconstruction (the duplicated positional is treated as a filename) while still writing correct output; running `/app/cmd.sh` directly is clean.

